many films have been made about Elvis  Presley but bazlerman's 2022 biopic is  the first to tackle the king's lonely  final years  baz luhrmann's elaborates often  anachronistic visual Panache always gets  a lot of attention whether he's  depicting 1890s Paris or 1920s New York  Elvis is no exception with its whirling  roulette wheel camera work and Hip-Hop  remixes of Presley songs but the film's  most daring conceit is arguably the way  it tells the story of Elvis from the  vantage point of Colonel Tom Parker  played by Tom Hanks under a mountain of  Prosthetics and a thick Dutch accent  Parker looks back on his life with the  King as he lies dying in a Vegas  hospital bed in 1997 wandering through a  spectral Casino as the memories come  flooding back I am the man who gave the  world Elvis Presley over the years since  Presley's death Parker has widely come  to be seen as a villain manipulating his  one and only client's trust and taking  far more than a share of the profits  lerman's film doesn't disagree with this  version of The Facts but in Parker's  mind he is Elvis's other half the  Carnival Barker to his Sideshow free the  Snowman to his show man Colonel Tom  Parker was a Kearney pure and simple the  film's most pelling tension is between  the blameless almost victimized Way  Parker sees his own relationship with  Elvis compared with what we the audience  see happening on screen this tension is  exposed early in the film with a brief  flash to 1974 when Elvis collapses  backstage before a concert and Parker  makes it perfectly clear that the only  thing that matters is that he gets back  on his feet and performs  as the film enters its final Act we  revisit that moment in 1974 when Presley  collapses with added context at this  point Elvis has been performing at the  International Hotel in Las Vegas for  years due to the fact that Parker signed  him in to essentially a lifetime  contract in exchange for the Forgiveness  of Parker's massive gambling debts if I  was you I would book it for a hundred  years  [Applause]  well no better time than the present  Parker has also been promising Presley  his first Global tour going back to the  1968 Comeback Special only to  continually delay the start of the tour  for one reason or another the truth  finally comes to light just moments  before the king collapses Parker keeps  delaying the tour because he came to the  United States illegally and doesn't have  a passport his name isn't even Tom  Parker nor was he ever a colonel but I  hear rumors  that Colonel is an alien after his  collapse Presley is given a shot of  amphetamines to get him up and going  again for the show he takes the stage  inebriated and incensed by his manager's  betrayal and in spectacular dramatic  fashion fires Parker in the middle of  his performance  you are fired  it's a deeply satisfying moment even if  it is nothing more than a fantasy  according to Elvis biographer Elena Nash  Presley and Parker's professional  falling out occurred after a show not in  the midst of one and for a completely  different reason furthermore Parker's  immigrant status wouldn't come to light  until after Presley's death there's no  evidence that the king ever suspected  that Parker was anyone other than the  West Virginia native he claimed to be  Parker's counter move however is  accurate to real life after being fired  by Presley Parker retires to his office  and drafts an invoice for unpaid  promotional work dating back to 1955  totaling over 8 million dollars  according to Nash this is based on a  real incident though the exact dollar  amount to the invoice is unknown Elvis's  father Vernon breaks the news to his son  that Parker's bill would bankrupt them  we're gonna lose Graceland  Elvis Furious storms out of his sweets  only to find Parker waiting for him  outside the two men argue viciously  Elvis gnashes teeth and threatens to  kill Parker the colonel in turn  belittles Presley's career reminding him  unfairly but honestly that The Comeback  Special audience that loved him so much  was a plant prompted to cheer by a  flashing Applause sign the scene is in  many ways the climax of the film and  Butler and Hanks give it their all Hanks  delivers the film's theme in the scene  declaring we are the same you and I  we are two heart lonely children  Reaching For Eternity his entreat falls  on deaf ears as Elvis walks out of the  Colonel's life forever  the film Fades the black with a title  card one year later Suddenly It's 1975  and Elvis is now a media curiosity a  real-life version of Sideshow act Parker  always considered him to be the colonel  dressed up the hotel like a carnival  there were posters teddy bears and Hound  dogs all over the place he's gained  weight he's separated from his white  Priscilla and rarely sees his daughter  Lisa Marie and when he's not performing  in Las Vegas he's holed up like a  permanent Graceland there is one glimmer  of hope as talks are apparently ongoing  to have him star with Barbara Streisand  and a remake of A Star is Born though we  know it will never come to be on the  tarmac alongside the private jet he  named after his daughter Elvis and  Priscilla have a tender conversation  like most of the dialogue in the film  its primary focus is to establish for us  the state of Elvis's life and career in  this moment and what his dreams and  goals are Elvis still wants to make one  good movie after dozens of cheap  musicals and worries that nothing he's  done will amount to anything when he  dies  and nobody's gonna remember me  I'll never do anything lasting the film  presents this as Elvis's final  conversation with his ex-wife although  in reality he would live for another two  years  from that scene on the tarmac the film  skips ahead two years to the  announcement that Presley has been found  dead in Graceland at the age of 42.  rather than try to recreate the  spectacle of Elvis's funeral procession  or the massive outpouring of public  grief that followed Lerman uses archival  footage of the real thing he also  includes an audio excerpt from President  Jimmy Carter's statement on Presley's  death in which he said his following was  immense and he was a symbol to people  the world over for the vitality  rebelliousness and Good Humor in this  country not too shabby for a kid who  scandalized the nation just two decades  earlier and choosing not to recreate  Elvis's funeral the film also skips over  the fact that Parker reportedly wore  Hawaiian shirts and baseball cap to the  service and while there trying to strike  deals with mourners according to  biographer James Dickerson we'll never  know whether his behavior came out of  callousness sociopathy denial or some  misguided sense of Showmanship just to  keep everyone talking about him as a  nation Mourns the loss of the king the  film flashes back to Presley's final  perform just weeks before his death  Lerman has thus far been reluctant to  make Butler up as the so-called fat  Elvis but here the young actor is decked  out in a wide pair of jowls and double  chin mumbling and stumbling as he sits  down at the piano and belts out an  emotional rendition of the Righteous  Brothers Unchained Melody sweat pouring  down his face the camera stays tight on  Butler in an intimate slightly  uncomfortable way it's different from  the way any of the film's previous  musical numbers have been shot but soon  we understand the reason why as Lerman  cuts from Butler as Elvis to footage of  the king himself singing accompanied by  a short Montage of the real-life Elvis  in many of the situations we've seen  dramatized in the film it's an  emotionally affecting transition as we  see just how accurately Lerman and  Butler have recreated so many moments in  Presley's life it's also a reminder of  just how powerful Elvis's voice and  presence were even in his final days he  gives a showstopper performance at that  piano and the audience hangs on every  note we still do  in the end Elvis can't speak for himself  so Parker does it for him as he did for  most of their life together Parker  Ponders who or what killed Elvis as he  shuffles about his purgatorial casino  floor in a hospital gown dragging an IV  pole it wasn't the pills and drugs  administered by doctors who should have  known better enabled by Parker Vernon  and Presley's whole Entourage the  so-called Memphis Mafia who were all  complicit in working Elvis to death what  can you do for him Dr Nick  and it wasn't Parker's greed and  mismanagement of Elvis's business which  forced him to continue performing at an  unforgiving pace for years Parker is all  too quick to absolve himself of that  responsibility  I made Elvis Presley now of course it's  we who killed Elvis with our Relentless  love for him and his inability to say no  Parker's monologue here is a fascinating  moment in the film Lerman and Hanks keep  Colonel Parker at arm's length unwilling  to tell the audience how we should feel  about him instead they let the dying  Dutch Carney the Snowman SnoWest to the  very end  after the screen Fades to Black we get a  series of postscripts filling Us in on  how The Saga of Elvis and The Colonel  played out in the years after Presley's  death the film takes Liberties in regard  to what Presley and the rest of the  world knew about Parker's background in  real life it would be 1980 before it  would be confirmed that Parker was of  Dutch descent and entered the country  illegally as a 30 year old man  eventually though Parker's Financial  malfeasance would come to light and in  1982 after a series of lawsuits filed by  Elvis Presley Enterprises a judge ruled  that Parker had no legal claim to any  parts of the Presley estate in his final  two decades Parker spent his days in Las  Vegas puttering around one casino floor  or another or in his immense wealth down  the drain he made few public appearances  or statements emerging from the Shadows  only on rare occasions such as the 1993  release of the Elvis postage stamp  Presley meanwhile far from the Forgotten  Lounge singer he feared he'd become  remains an international Superstar  nearly 50 years after his death and one  of the highest earning dead celebrities  of all time his life and work still  Inspire admiration and Mercy  [Music]

